<p>If 2023 was where Michael's dynasty was born, Two Thousand Twenty-Five was
where it stopped being an argument. He went <span class="stat">12–3</span>, ran off
<span class="stat">six straight</span>, and put the whole thing to bed. But the
season everyone will actually remember belongs to a man who finished
<em>dead last</em> — because 2025 is the year the football gods singled out one
owner for a season of pure, uncut cruelty, and his name is Richard.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The League's Greatest Tragedy</h3>

<div class="decree">Richard scored 1,711.20 points in 2025 — the third-most in
the entire league — and finished 4–11, dead last, in the toilet, alone.</div>

<p>Read that again. <strong>Richard</strong>'s <em>Doener</em> put up
<span class="stat">1,711.20</span> — more than the eventual runner-up, more than
two of the four playoff teams — and the schedule handed him <span class="stat">4–11</span>
and the wooden spoon. By the all-play he was a <span class="stat">74–61</span>
team, a <span class="stat">.548</span> outfit that in a fair universe makes the
playoffs comfortably; the gap between what he earned and what he got —
<span class="stat">−0.281</span> — is the largest single-season robbery in the
recorded history of this league. He dropped a <span class="stat">142.72</span> in
Week 15, a top-three score that week, and lost it. Every Sunday he loaded the
cannon, and every Sunday the league sat him next to whoever happened to be having
the week of their life. It is the single best piece of material this archive owns,
and it is not funny, it is <em>tragic</em>, and Richard deserved better. He will
not get an apology from the standings. Nobody ever does.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Fraud and the Beneficiaries</h3>

<p>While Richard was being crucified, others were getting away with murder.
<strong>Jeremy</strong> — <em>Cook Brown Rice in Rome</em>, and yes, still the
doctor — turned a <span class="stat">70–65</span> all-play into a
<span class="stat">10–5</span> record and a third-place check, the biggest luck
gap in the league at <span class="stat">+0.148</span>. <strong>David</strong> was
right behind him, laundering a <span class="stat">.459</span> squad into
<span class="stat">9–6</span> — the German got the season Richard should have
had, and if he objected to <em>that</em> he kept it to himself for once.
<strong>Bates</strong> named his team <em>Carried by Gibbs*</em> — asterisk his,
not ours — and then went out and proved it, riding <span class="stat">Jahmyr
Gibbs</span> (including a <span class="stat">49.90</span> Week 12, the best
single-player game of the year) all the way to a runner-up finish and
<span class="stat">$875</span>. When a man tells you in his own team name that
one player is carrying him, believe him.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Commissioner Eats It</h3>

<p>And then, gloriously, there is <strong>Cory</strong>. After the 2023 heist and
the 2024 near-miss, the commissioner's luck finally ran out, and he named the team
that would suffer it <em>Scheisse</em> — German for exactly what the season was.
He went <span class="stat">5–10</span>, finished seventh, and won
<span class="stat">$0</span>, and he did it while leaving another
<span class="stat">245</span> points on his own bench, because some things are
constant across all seasons and all fortunes: the tides, the sunrise, and Cory
setting the wrong lineup. The man who two years earlier stole a playoff berth with
the worst team in the money got handed a plate of Scheisse and ate every bite.
The website he built to track everyone's dollars dutifully recorded his: none.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Rest of the Field</h3>

<p><strong>Dylan</strong> — <em>Schmelley</em>, forever — went
<span class="stat">8–7</span> and missed again, posting the
<em>worst lineup efficiency in the league</em> at <span class="stat">84.3%</span>
while leaving a season-worst <span class="stat">308</span> points on his bench.
The hump remains un-gotten-over; the hump has, at this point, filed for
squatter's rights. <strong>Marian</strong>'s <em>Show me your TDs</em> scored the
third-most points in the league and finished <span class="stat">7–8</span>, robbed
by <span class="stat">−0.104</span> — a lesser tragedy only because Richard
existed to make everyone else's bad luck look like a rounding error.
<strong>Justin</strong> and <strong>Sam</strong> rounded out the 5–10 middle-class
of the damned, Sam's <em>Golden Girls</em> scraping the league's lowest
points-for. Everybody had a reason to complain. Only one of them earned it.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Champion</h3>

<div class="respect">
  <p class="crown">The One Paragraph of Respect</p>
  <p><strong>Michael</strong> — <em>Dixie Wrecked</em> — went
  <span class="stat">12–3</span> and made it look routine, which is the surest
  sign of a real one. A <span class="stat">90–45</span> all-play record, a
  six-game win streak, the top seed wire to wire, and a second ring in three years
  to go with the 2023 title — this is a bona fide dynasty now, not a hot streak,
  and the money says so: <span class="stat">$1,325</span> in a single season, the
  richest year any owner has posted in the box-score era. When the rest of the
  league is arguing about who got robbed, Michael is the answer to the only
  question that pays: who won. Again.</p>
</div>

<p>So 2025 closes with the dynasty confirmed, the doctor cashing a lucky check,
the commissioner eating Scheisse, and Richard — poor, cursed, magnificent Richard
— scoring more than almost anyone alive and finishing beneath all of them. The
money table is below. For once, it is not the cruelest thing on the page.</p>
